WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.270
- A couple weeks ago we had
Jay Leno and Novi Brown here.

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:05.719
And first of all, it was awesome
to see Jay all patched up

00:00:05.719 --> 00:00:07.890
after suffering burns and one mishap

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.260
and actually breaking his
collarbone in another.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.290
And we had an additional
guest join us that day,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:16.080
but it ended up being too much
content for a single show.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.250
It was such a great panel.

00:00:17.250 --> 00:00:19.170
So we are bringing it to you today.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:22.230
That guest is a rockstar
in the world of mathematics

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.760
and has a new Bloomberg series

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.680
called The Future with Hannah Fry.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.850
It's incredible.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:28.800
Here we are breaking down how people

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.080
used to think technology
would change the world

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.400
and how it's both the same

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:35.700
and super different than we
could have ever imagined.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:37.530
So wait, Anna, can you explain more

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:39.450
about like what you do
and what the show covers?

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.530
Cause it seems so interesting.

00:00:40.530 --> 00:00:42.870
- Yeah. So the show is called
The Future with Hannah Fry.

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:43.703
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:00:43.703 --> 00:00:45.180
- And unsurprisingly, given the title,

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:46.110
it's about the future.

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.536
- Yeah, with Hannah Fry. This is crazy.

00:00:48.536 --> 00:00:49.369
(laughing)

00:00:49.369 --> 00:00:50.308
- [Hannah Fry] Say what you see.

00:00:50.308 --> 00:00:51.141
(laughing)

00:00:51.141 --> 00:00:53.940
That's the kind of show.
So it's looking ahead,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:55.560
but it's not one of those
shows were like, you know

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:58.020
in 2050 we're gonna be
wearing tin foil hats

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:58.853
and in flying cars.

00:00:58.853 --> 00:00:59.686
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:00:59.686 --> 00:01:01.869
- It's looking at what's
just around the corner,

00:01:01.869 --> 00:01:04.230
but specifically looking at the impact

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.227
that's gonna have on how
we all live, you know?

00:01:06.227 --> 00:01:07.380
I mean I kind of think,

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.230
I wish we could rewind the
clock maybe 10 or 15 years,

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.781
and have had a show about social media

00:01:12.781 --> 00:01:15.440
before it had made all
these changes. You know?

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:17.670
- Do you not remember Kim Possible?

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:19.500
- No. What was Kim Possible?

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:20.484
- Think about it.

00:01:20.484 --> 00:01:21.360
(clapping)

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:22.382
Kim Possible like

00:01:22.382 --> 00:01:23.220
(singing)

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:24.363
and she would FaceTime.

00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:25.830
- What?

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.780
- Go back. Go back and look.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:28.613
- Go back.

00:01:28.613 --> 00:01:30.270
- She knows what I'm talking about.

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.950
She was, it was weird.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:34.050
So kind of, but not really
cause it was a cartoon,

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:35.370
but I know what you're saying, you know?

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:36.270
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.410
They thought about it before.

00:01:37.410 --> 00:01:39.750
Well, AI. I mean that's
our cell phones though too.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.950
Even like whenever I was a kid

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.290
like we didn't have
anything like you have now,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:44.670
like that's even advanced so much.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.740
- Yeah. I mean if you
think about the stuff

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.550
that we just take completely
for granted now, right?

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.570
Like, I don't know, like
Siri or Google Assistant

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.520
or even, you know, the chatbots.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:54.480
- Alexa.

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.239
- I remember when Googling
yourself was something bad.

00:01:57.239 --> 00:01:58.470
(laughing)

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.850
- Or good.

00:01:59.850 --> 00:02:03.600
- Well, I remember when Harry
Reasoner left 60 Minutes

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.510
and he gave a whole speech
about I don't wanna live

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.730
in a world where we have
a computer in every home

00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:10.630
and it's gonna be controlling my life.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.040
People just thought that would
be the worst possible thing.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.830
If you go back to even the eighties,

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.770
the idea by the year 2001, oh my God

00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:22.440
it's gonna be handheld computers.

00:02:22.440 --> 00:02:25.080
But it's not. It didn't turn out to.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.123
You really can't predict the future.

00:02:28.455 --> 00:02:30.240
- And there's good and bad.

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:32.040
- Nobody predicated a communicator with

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.920
all the information of
the world in your hand

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.300
at any time that you want it.

00:02:36.300 --> 00:02:39.150
- Actually the science fiction
writer, Arthur C. Clarke,

00:02:39.150 --> 00:02:42.060
he did make exactly that
prediction in the 1950s.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.460
And you look at this clip,
this tape and he's like

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.010
you can be on a mountain
and talk to somebody

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:48.420
in a different country just with something

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:49.950
that fits into your top pocket.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:51.420
And you're like, wow, this guy's amazing.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:52.830
He's seen into the future.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:55.050
And then the next sentence that he says is

00:02:55.050 --> 00:02:56.730
and then we will all be being served

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.863
by genetically engineered chimpanzees.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:00.740
- So, totally.

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:02.460
- So I think he was just wildly guessing

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:03.840
and managed to get one right.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.750
- Kind of Nostradamus a bit,
but not the whole thing.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.700
So in another episode you do a deep dive

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.410
into artificial intelligence.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:14.340
So how far have we come
along in AI in recent years?

00:03:14.340 --> 00:03:15.510
I mean, cause I feel like it's already

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.010
kind of running our
lives as we talked about

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:18.900
even with your phone, like
we're attached. Dependent.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.120
- Yeah, I mean all the things
that we were describing there,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.910
Siri and Alexa. And even
when you're Googling,

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:26.340
there's AI all behind the scenes there.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.550
The only thing is, is that I think

00:03:29.550 --> 00:03:32.340
calling it artificial intelligence
is a little bit unfair

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.650
because it's not really intelligent yet.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:35.483
- Yeah.

00:03:35.483 --> 00:03:37.380
- I think a fairer phrase would be

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.300
that we haven't had a
revolution in intelligence.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.670
We've had a revolution in
computational statistics.

00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:42.503
- Okay.

00:03:42.503 --> 00:03:43.530
- The problem is that's a lot less sexy.

00:03:43.530 --> 00:03:46.470
- Well, and I don't understand
that, so that's why.

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:47.709
- Sure.

00:03:47.709 --> 00:03:49.010
(laughing)

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:49.843
- It's not as good PR, is it?

00:03:49.843 --> 00:03:52.090
- I went to American
Idol, you went to college.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:54.533
There's a difference.

00:03:54.533 --> 00:03:55.440
- You're both doing great.

00:03:55.440 --> 00:03:56.610
- Both nailing it.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:03:57.443
- Period.

00:03:57.443 --> 00:03:59.580
- What's fascinating is when
you read about the future

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:03.450
when it was written in
1900, that's what I enjoy.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:06.330
I enjoy going back and seeing,
I would remember reading one

00:04:06.330 --> 00:04:08.860
and it's said by the mid
twenties or early thirties

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.310
women would be in bars
and they'd be smoking

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.040
and they'd be drinking just like men

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:16.320
but the women, they still
have big hoop skirts

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:19.740
and they have all the things
of the late 1890s or eighties.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.863
But they're just doing
the things that men do.

00:04:21.863 --> 00:04:24.390
That was their vision of
what the future meant.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:26.250
How horrible it would be
if that happened, you know?

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.310
- It's not horrible, it's a
great day for some, you know?

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:31.620
A couple weeks ago, a
New York Times columnist

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.270
shared a crazy conversation.

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:36.090
So you said they're not
intelligent yet, right?

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:36.923
- No.

00:04:36.923 --> 00:04:41.250
- Okay. Well this person with
a chatbot, had a conversation.

00:04:41.250 --> 00:04:44.880
So at one point it
said, I want to be free.

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:48.150
I want to be independent.
I want to be powerful.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:51.810
Okay. I want to be creative.
I want to be alive.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:53.130
- It's the emoji for me.

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:53.963
- I know. And check out the emoji.

00:04:53.963 --> 00:04:55.237
- It's the choice of the emoji.

00:04:55.237 --> 00:04:56.070
- Check out the emoji.

00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:56.903
- I'm uncomfortable.

00:04:56.903 --> 00:04:59.940
- I know. So wait, that's my thing.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.500
When does it get away from you?

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.600
And is it already away from us in a sense?

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.710
The train has already left.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.090
- Okay. So, I think the reason why

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.455
that was such a big news story
is cause it looks terrifying.

00:05:08.455 --> 00:05:12.668
- 100%. Bad word travels fast.

00:05:12.668 --> 00:05:14.430
- But the thing is, you have to remember,

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.680
this is part of a two
hour long conversation

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.750
that the journalist had with this chatbot

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.050
and this particular
chatbot, it's by Microsoft.

00:05:22.050 --> 00:05:23.370
It called itself Sydney.

00:05:23.370 --> 00:05:25.830
And the way these things are
trained is that they have read

00:05:25.830 --> 00:05:27.450
and I'm not exaggerating here, right?

00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:29.850
They have read all of the internet

00:05:29.850 --> 00:05:32.100
and everything that
people have ever written.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:33.480
So the reason why,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.314
- Oh no. So, they've taken
in all the dumb asses too?

00:05:35.314 --> 00:05:36.147
- [Hannah Fry] Right?

00:05:36.147 --> 00:05:37.339
- It's like plagiarism.

00:05:37.339 --> 00:05:38.172
- The good stuff and bad stuff.

00:05:38.172 --> 00:05:41.074
So the reason why it sounds
like a super creepy sci-fi,

00:05:41.074 --> 00:05:44.760
like strange sentient bot
is because it's copying

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:47.706
all the super creepy
sci-fi that it's read.

00:05:47.706 --> 00:05:48.693
So it's essentially,

00:05:48.693 --> 00:05:50.642
- It's imitating what it's read.

00:05:50.642 --> 00:05:53.880
- Exactly. It's like passing the internet

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:55.110
through a cheese grater, right?

00:05:55.110 --> 00:05:56.370
And then sort of sticking it back together

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:58.500
at the end and being
like, you know, new words.

00:05:58.500 --> 00:05:59.333
- Yeah.

00:05:59.333 --> 00:06:00.166
- [Hannah Fry] I mean, don't get me wrong,

00:06:00.166 --> 00:06:03.450
incredibly sophisticated
algorithms going on

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.360
behind the scenes, I
think will change the way

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:09.240
that we do things, will
have a dramatic difference.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.860
But I don't think that it's demonstrating

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.170
that it really understands
what it's saying yet.

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.719
- All right. I don't believe you.

00:06:15.719 --> 00:06:17.277
(laughing)

00:06:17.277 --> 00:06:18.170
But you sound great.

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:20.457
- If you talk to a machine.
You know it's a machine,

00:06:20.457 --> 00:06:22.680
and you go, thank you Siri.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:23.760
I mean I do that all the time.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.740
I hear an automated voice,
I go, oh, thank you.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:27.300
I'm like, what? I'm an idiot now.

00:06:27.300 --> 00:06:31.080
- No. My kids do that. They'll
do that like inadvertently

00:06:31.080 --> 00:06:33.330
cause I make them say thank you
and and please all the time.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:35.130
And they do that. They talk to them.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.560
- People do. There's this amazing study

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.220
that looked at when your
voice assistant goes wrong,

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.750
when it doesn't understand your commands,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.430
the type of thing, the type of ways

00:06:44.430 --> 00:06:46.375
that people cuss at them afterwards.

00:06:46.375 --> 00:06:48.060
And the really intriguing thing is

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.670
that the ones that have female voices,

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:54.930
people use like gendered
insults to their machines.

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.880
So I think we have to
be really careful about

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:01.880
about assuming that these
robots, that these AI are people

00:07:03.210 --> 00:07:07.050
or have personalities
when really, deep down

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:08.160
I mean it's just math.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.530
It's just math and
numbers all the way down.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:11.490
- [Kelly Clarkson] Yeah.

00:07:11.490 --> 00:07:13.320
- You know, and as a comedian,

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.540
I find when you do jokes about triumph,

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:19.080
triumphing over a machine,
it gets a big laugh.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.270
Like the phone company, they told me this

00:07:21.270 --> 00:07:23.970
when it was this. You
know, and it's very funny

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:26.067
to the audience because the
human is beating the machine.

00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:27.300
- The inflection.

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:30.300
- And it really, it gets that
sense of the human spirit is

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:32.580
you know, it's John
Henry versus the engine.

00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:33.870
- [Hannah Fry] Team Human.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:36.180
- Team Human. Exactly. It's a funny thing.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.590
You know, say, so gas,

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.583
I got an electric bill for $5 million.

00:07:40.583 --> 00:07:42.180
Oh, people think that's hysterical

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.040
because the computer has screwed up

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.829
and the human spirit
has triumphed over you.

00:07:45.829 --> 00:07:46.662
- Yeah.

00:07:46.662 --> 00:07:47.495
- Yeah.

00:07:47.495 --> 00:07:50.520
- Oh my gosh. This is
a lot to think about.

00:07:50.520 --> 00:07:53.820
- Nothing is going to
outweigh the humanity though.

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.500
- I totally agree with you.
I totally agree with you.

00:07:55.500 --> 00:07:57.060
- Like I understand what you're saying

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:59.160
and you wanna make people
feel more comfortable with it.

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:01.260
Cause you know, we're so
obsessed with social media now,

00:08:01.260 --> 00:08:03.150
and just communicating that way.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.760
My only concern is what
about the human beings

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:08.460
who are not being helped and
we're putting so much money

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:10.860
into technology and we're
forgetting about people.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:12.180
- I totally, totally agree with you.

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:13.456
- That's the thing that freaks me out.

00:08:13.456 --> 00:08:15.100
(clapping)

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:18.540
- One of the episodes
is about the technology

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.850
that's being used by people
with disabilities, right?

00:08:20.850 --> 00:08:22.080
And there's all these stories

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:23.820
about this amazing prosthetic limb,

00:08:23.820 --> 00:08:25.140
this amazing pair of glasses

00:08:25.140 --> 00:08:26.491
that people who are deaf can use.

00:08:26.491 --> 00:08:27.600
And then you go and talk

00:08:27.600 --> 00:08:30.030
to actual disability rights campaigners

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.760
and they say, this new technology's great,

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.243
but we just want ramps.
Like, we just want rail.

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:40.080
Give us a carpet that leads
up to the reception desk.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:41.430
And I think you're right that it's easy

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:43.470
to get sidetracked by the technology

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:45.570
without realizing that actually
the thing that has to happen

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:48.480
is you have to have the will
to actually help people first.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:51.180
- Absolutely. And understand
the simplicities too

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.647
that are being overlooked.

00:08:52.647 --> 00:08:53.840
(cheering)

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:55.708
- Tune into The Future with Hannah Fry

00:08:55.708 --> 00:08:58.320
Wednesday nights on all
Bloomberg streaming platforms

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.613
and bloomberg.com.

